---
// ait.astro — the case-study page for IssueSight (the Agentic Issue Tracker,
// "AIT"). Lives at "/projects/ait/". Uses ProjectLayout for the frame.
//
// Images live in  public/projects/ait/  and are referenced as
// "/projects/ait/<file>.png". Demo videos are unlisted YouTube embeds.
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

// The shared TypeScript "contract" my partner and I agreed on before writing
// any code. Kept here as a plain string so the angle brackets render as text
// (in .astro, a bare "<" in the template would be read as an HTML tag).
const contractCode = `// The data shapes both halves of the system agreed on up front.

export interface OTelSpan {
  sessionId:      string;
  operationName:  string;   // 'llm_call', 'tool_call', etc.
  model:          string;
  inputTokens:    number;
  outputTokens:   number;
  thinkingTokens: number;
  durationMs:     number;
}

export interface ToolInfo {
  toolName:    string;        // e.g. "run_shell_command", "read_file"
  command:     string;        // the actual command / args passed
  description: string;        // what the tool does
  output:      string;        // what the tool returned
  timestamp:   string;        // when it was called
  isCommit:    boolean;       // was this a git commit?
  commitSha:   string | null; // extracted SHA if it was
}

export interface SessionContext {
  sessionId:      string;
  cwd:            string;
  repo:           string | null;
  issueId:        number | null;
  models:         Set<string>;
  inputTokens:    number;
  outputTokens:   number;
  thinkingTokens: number;
  totalCost:      number;
  startedAt:      string;
  startCommitSha: string | null;
  toolCalls:      ToolInfo[];
  agentCommits:   string[];
}

// The session manager's job, defined as an interface before either of us
// started implementing — so the receiver and the manager couldn't drift.
export interface SessionManager {
  createSession(sessionId: string, cwd: string): void;
  onSpanReceived(sessionId: string, span: OTelSpan): void;
  updateTokens(sessionId: string, span: OTelSpan): void;
  updateToolCalls(sessionId: string, callInfo: ToolInfo): void;
  claimIssue(sessionId: string, issueId: number): void;
  finalizeSession(sessionId: string): void;
  getActiveSession(sessionId: string): SessionContext | null;
  getAllActiveSessions(): SessionContext[];
}`;
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="IssueSight"
  tagline="An agentic issue tracker (AIT), built as a VS Code extension. I designed and built the agent-detection backend — the telemetry receiver that measures exactly what an AI coding agent does on a GitHub issue."
  status="Team project"
  tags={[
    "OpenTelemetry",
    "Gemini CLI",
    "Node.js",
    "VS Code Extension",
    "Jest",
  ]}
  url="https://cse110-sp26-group02.github.io/cse110-sp26-project-site/"
>
  {/* ---------------- OVERVIEW ---------------- */}
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    IssueSight is a VS Code&ndash;native <strong>Agentic Issue Tracker (AIT)</strong>:
    a GitHub issue tracker that also measures what an AI coding agent actually
    did while working on each issue &mdash; which models it used, how many tokens
    it burned, how long it took, which commits it authored, and which tools it
    ran. It was a team project (eleven people) for a software-engineering
    course, built as a VS Code extension over a multi-week sprint.
  </p>
  <p>
    I worked on the <strong>agent-detection backend</strong>: the research behind how
    we detect agent activity at all, the local telemetry <em>receiver</em> that
    captures it, the auto-setup that wires an agent up to report to us, and the
    early product demos we used to get stakeholder feedback. The team repo is
    private, so this page is the walkthrough of what I built and why.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- THE PROBLEM ---------------- */}
  <h2>The problem: measuring an agent deterministically</h2>
  <p>
    The hard question wasn&rsquo;t the UI &mdash; it was detection. When a
    developer points an AI coding agent at a repository, how do you reliably know
    <em>which repo</em> and <em>which issue</em> it&rsquo;s working on, and how do
    you capture <em>exactly</em> the model and token usage of every call without
    trusting the model to self-report? Early on, the whole sub-team&rsquo;s
    concern was <strong>determinism</strong>: we wanted ground-truth data, not
    probabilistic guesses.
  </p>
  <p>
    I did the initial research and proposed the architecture we ended up building
    on. The core idea: capture data from two reliable, local sources instead of
    prompting the model to describe itself.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenTelemetry (OTel)</strong> for the hard numbers &mdash; the exact
      model and token counts emitted on every LLM call, streamed to a local HTTP
      server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gemini CLI hooks</strong> (<code>SessionStart</code> /
      <code>SessionEnd</code> / <code>AfterToolUse</code>) for lifecycle, tool
      use, and commits &mdash; plus the <code>cwd</code> exposed in the hook
      environment, which lets us read the local git remote to identify the repo
      with zero manual entry.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is the first architecture I proposed in our early team discussions,
    while we were still weighing how deterministic our detection could be:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/inital-diagram.png"
      alt="Initial proposed architecture diagram from the team's first discussions, sketching how OTel spans and Gemini hooks feed a local receiver that tracks sessions, repos, commits, and tokens."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The initial proposal from our first discussions &mdash; OTel plus Gemini
      hooks feeding a local receiver, keyed by session.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Sessions are keyed by a unique session id in an in-memory map, so multiple
    concurrent agent sessions accumulate independently. Issues map to sessions by
    a user &ldquo;claiming&rdquo; one, and in the design, finished sessions would
    be persisted to SQLite. (In practice, the MVP we shipped tracked everything
    live from that in-memory map &mdash; more on that scope shift below.) That
    firmed up into this fuller picture of the system:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/rough-architecture.png"
      alt="Architecture diagram: the user drives an AI coding agent, which reports OTel spans to a local OTLP receiver inside the VS Code extension host; the extension pulls issues from the GitHub API, stores data in local SQLite, and renders a sidebar dashboard."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The system at a glance: the agent reports passively via telemetry; the
      extension host runs the receiver, talks to GitHub, stores to SQLite, and
      drives the sidebar UI.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- RESEARCH / ADR ---------------- */}
  <h2>Research and the key architectural decision</h2>
  <p>
    Before writing code, the team ran a real decision process and recorded
    Architectural Decision Records (ADRs) in MADR format. A few foundational ones
    shaped everything I built:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>We&rsquo;re a tracker, not a solver.</strong> Rather than mirror
      tools like Linear that delegate issues to an agent to solve, we chose to
      strictly track and prep issues. That kept ownership (and blame) with the
      human, and dropped a huge amount of permission/complexity from scope.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remove &ldquo;Create&rdquo; from CRUD.</strong> GitHub stays the one
      source of truth for creating issues; we handle read/update/delete. Less UI,
      no write-access to the repo, cleaner data integrity.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ship as a VS Code extension.</strong> It sits in the developer&rsquo;s
      real workflow, gives us a proper filesystem and SQLite (vs. a web
      app&rsquo;s <code>localStorage</code>), and direct CLI access for git.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/adr-index.png"
      alt="Index table of Architectural Decision Records, each with an ID, title, and 'Accepted' status — including GitHub Issues as provider, Gemini as LLM provider, VS Code extension platform, SQLite, tracker-not-solver, and telemetry-based agent tracking."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Our ADR index &mdash; every significant technical decision documented with
      its context, the alternatives we rejected, and why.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The decision that governed my work was <strong>&ldquo;Telemetry + Gemini
    Hooks based Agent tracking.&rdquo;</strong> We weighed nine options &mdash;
    probabilistic behavior matching, artifact pre-prompting, a LangFuse-style
    wrapper, GitHub Agentic Workflows, restricting models with manual pricing, a
    LiteLLM local proxy, scraping agent logs, VS Code debug logs, and VS Code
    OpenTelemetry. We chose the combination of <strong>Gemini hooks + Gemini
    telemetry</strong> because it gives precise, ground-truth data that emerges
    directly from the user&rsquo;s own machine with minimal setup. The main
    trade-off we accepted: for the MVP, it ties us to a single provider (Gemini).
    That decision let us reliably capture, per tracked issue: commits (by agent
    vs. human), models used, tokens in/out, prompt duration, and the tools the
    agent invoked.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- COMMIT TRACKING ---------------- */}
  <h2>Detecting commits: two approaches, combined</h2>
  <p>
    Commit attribution was the sub-problem where my partner Sahil and I initially
    diverged &mdash; and where combining our ideas made the design noticeably
    better. I first explored a <strong>start/end commit</strong> approach: record
    the <code>HEAD</code> SHA at <code>SessionStart</code> and
    <code>SessionEnd</code>, then compute every commit in between. I recorded an
    early exploration of that:
  </p>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/U0jKCAKA9ew"
      title="Start/end commit tracking exploration"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p>
    Sahil&rsquo;s approach used an <code>AfterToolUse</code> hook to catch commits
    live, as the agent runs them. We met, compared trade-offs, and made an
    executive decision to <strong>combine both</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>AfterToolUse hooks</strong> give live, agent-specific tracking (and,
      as a bonus, visibility into every other tool the agent runs &mdash; what it
      read, what it executed) &mdash; but miss human commits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Start/end commit diffing</strong> catches <em>every</em> commit
      regardless of author &mdash; but only resolves once the session ends.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So the finalized pipeline is: OTel for tokens and the exact model on each LLM
    call; hooks for the session lifecycle, tool calls, live agent commits, and the
    <code>cwd</code>/repo; and a <strong>post-session reconciliation</strong> that
    diffs start-to-end commits and subtracts the known agent commits to isolate
    the human ones. Since we were already living in the hook system, AfterToolUse
    was a natural fit, and it&rsquo;s the most honest representation of the
    product&rsquo;s purpose &mdash; tracking what the <em>agent</em> did &mdash;
    while still letting a developer see their own commits after the fact.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- ARCHITECTURE / FLOW ---------------- */}
  <h2>How the pieces fit together</h2>
  <p>
    With the decisions locked, we mapped the full runtime flow &mdash; setup,
    session start, and everything that fires during a session:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/shared-diagram.png"
      alt="Two-phase flow diagram. Phase 1 (setup): setup.js writes .gemini config and hooks, starts the HTTP server, claims a pending issue, and starts the receiver. Phase 2 (tracking): each agent action — session start, prompts, output spans, tool use, session end, issue switch — maps to a receiver function such as createSession, onSpanReceived, updateToolCalls, and finalizeSession."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The end-to-end flow, color-coded by responsibility (human work, logic, API,
      telemetry, UI, agent work).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- DIVIDING THE WORK ---------------- */}
  <h2>Dividing the work: designing the interface first</h2>
  <p>
    Sahil and I split the backend cleanly: I built the <strong>receiver </strong>
     (the HTTP server that ingests spans and hook events and parses them into our
    shared shapes), and he built the <strong>session manager</strong> (the state
    logic those parsed events drive). The thing I&rsquo;m happiest with is that we
    designed the <em>contract between the two halves before writing either one</em>
    &mdash; the data shapes and the exact function signatures &mdash; so our code
    couldn&rsquo;t drift out of sync.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/work-dist.png"
      alt="Sequence-style diagram splitting responsibilities: the Outside World fires hooks and spans, Scott (receiver) parses each into typed objects, and hands off to Sahil (session manager) functions like createSession, onSpanReceived/updateTokens, updateToolCalls, claimIssue, and finalizeSession."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The hand-off we agreed on: the receiver (mine) parses raw hooks/spans into
      typed objects and calls into the session manager (Sahil&rsquo;s).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The shared contract we committed to before implementing:</p>
  <pre><code>{contractCode}</code></pre>

  {/* ---------------- BUILDING THE RECEIVER ---------------- */}
  <h2>Building the receiver</h2>
  <p>
    I scaffolded the directory at
    <code>src/back/logic/agent-detection</code> &mdash; <code>agent_receiver.js</code>
    (the local HTTP server), <code>session_types.js</code> (the interfaces above),
    and a <code>helpers/</code> folder for OTel parsing, hook-event parsing, git,
    and cost calculations &mdash; and documented it in a directory README and the
    project&rsquo;s <code>STRUCTURE.md</code>. We followed the codebase practice of
    writing JSDoc and unit tests <em>as</em> we implemented, not after.
  </p>
  <p>
    The receiver stands up a local server that catches incoming OTel spans and
    Gemini hook events (<code>SessionStart</code>/<code>SessionEnd</code> and
    <code>AfterToolUse</code>), parses both into our unified
    <code>OTelSpan</code> and <code>ToolInfo</code> shapes, and routes them into
    the session-manager functions. A big part of getting it right was hardening
    the edge cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Ignore <code>user_prompt </code> spans that arrive with <strong>0 tokens </strong>
      so they don&rsquo;t pollute token counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Handle <strong>duplicate <code>SessionEnd</code> hooks</strong> without
      double-finalizing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Safely drop spans that arrive <strong>late</strong>, after a session has
      already closed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Here&rsquo;s the receiver running against a real Gemini session &mdash; a
    normal prompt, then a prompt that makes the agent use a tool, then a faked
    <code>AfterToolUse</code> payload with a <code>git commit</code> to exercise
    the <code>isCommit</code>/<code>commitSha</code> path, then quitting. You can
    see the <code>ignored_non_token_span</code> and
    <code>ignored_duplicate_session_end</code> guards firing:
  </p>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/_qYZb3JljJA"
      title="Receiver implementation test against a real Gemini session"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p>
    A receiver is useless if the agent isn&rsquo;t configured to report to it, so
    I also wrote an <strong>auto-setup helper </strong>
    (<code>gemini_settings_helper.js</code>) that creates/updates
    <code>.gemini/settings.json</code>, installs the hook functions, and enables
    OTel telemetry &mdash; while carefully preserving any unrelated settings the
    user already had. When the extension activates, it just calls
    <code>t2ensureGeminiSettings(cwd)</code> then
    <code>t2startAgentReceiver()</code>, and tears down with
    <code>t2stopAgentReceiver()</code> on deactivate.
  </p>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/hea4vlu0oEw"
      title="Automatic Gemini settings setup helper"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p>
    To validate the numbers weren&rsquo;t just plausible but <em>correct</em>, I
    wrote integration tests across the full receiver &rarr; session-manager flow
    and manually cross-checked our accumulated tokens against Gemini CLI&rsquo;s
    own end-of-session summary. The math matched exactly:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/token-val.png"
      alt="Side-by-side terminal output: Gemini CLI's interaction summary reports 23,102 input and 36 output tokens for a session, and the receiver's tracked session object shows the same inputTokens: 23102 and outputTokens: 36 highlighted."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Validation: the tokens my receiver accumulated from OTel spans (right) add
      up to exactly what Gemini CLI reports at session end (left).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    An earlier exploration, before the architecture settled, of just pulling token
    usage and the specific model out of the agent:
  </p>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/nZvQQgMKHsw"
      title="Early exploration of token usage and model detection"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>

  {/* ---------------- SESSION MGMT / BUGS ---------------- */}
  <h2>Session management, and the bugs worth remembering</h2>
  <p>
    On the session-manager side, Sahil implemented the live state &mdash;
    accumulating tokens, tracking active models, and logging commits &mdash; and
    we untangled a few good bugs together. The nastiest: the
    <code>session.id</code> in the OTel payload was actually the OTel
    <em>span&rsquo;s</em> id, while the real Gemini session id had to be pulled
    from <code>gen_ai.conversation.id</code>. The claim logic also had two modes
    worth calling out: claiming an issue for a session that doesn&rsquo;t exist yet
    marks it <em>pending</em> and binds when that session starts; claiming with no
    session id acts as a <em>wildcard</em> that binds to the next session to open.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later we deliberately <strong>refactored cost out of the session manager</strong>.
    Rather than silo pricing inside agent-detection, we kept per-model
    <em> token</em> tracking there (converting the <code>models</code> set into a
    map keyed by model, so switching models mid-session stays accurate) and left
    cost calculation to a separate layer &mdash; avoiding data duplication and
    keeping room for user-defined custom pricing as API prices shift.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- INTEGRATION TESTING ---------------- */}
  <h2>Integration testing: treating the webview as a black box</h2>
  <p>
    When we moved to testing the VS Code integration, a real design question came
    up: how do you verify a &ldquo;ping&rdquo; actually crosses the boundary
    between the WebView and the extension host? One option was to mock
    <code>vscode.window.showInformationMessage</code> and call the message handler
    directly. The other was to inject a small test-harness script into the
    webview&rsquo;s HTML and fire a <em>real</em>
    <code>acquireVsCodeApi().postMessage(...)</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We chose the second. The first option only tests an isolated handler function
    and bypasses the webview entirely; the second treats the WebView process as a
    black box and actually proves the wiring between two independent processes
    works &mdash; a true integration test. That&rsquo;s the version that gives you
    confidence the extension is really talking to its UI.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/cicd.png"
      alt="CI/CD pipeline flowchart: push to main triggers ESLint; on pass, docs generation and static-site deploy; then three parallel test suites (Jest unit tests, vscode-test extension-host tests, Playwright E2E); if all pass, the extension is packaged into a .vsix."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The CI/CD pipeline the project settled on: lint &rarr; docs/site deploy
      &rarr; parallel unit / extension-host / E2E tests &rarr; package to
      <code>.vsix</code>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- DEMOS ---------------- */}
  <h2>Shipping demos for feedback</h2>
  <p>
    Once the core detection worked, I pushed to get something <em>visible</em> in
    front of our stakeholder &mdash; the team had hit some hold-ups, and
    I strongly believed a rough, watchable demo would unblock feedback faster than
    waiting for a polished build. So I wired a quick, deliberately messy
    end-to-end slice: click an issue, wait for a session to start, and watch real
    tokens and models stream in and reconcile at session end.
  </p>
  <p>This was me pushing a product-first mindset on the team &mdash; in my own words to the group at the time:</p>
  <blockquote>
    &ldquo;I felt a bit concerned about how we might not have some product, even
    if super minimal or rough, to show. Even if we&rsquo;re graded on the
    &lsquo;process,&rsquo; it doesn&rsquo;t feel great to have literally nothing
    to show&hellip; The idea from class &mdash; that releasing a flawed,
    half-baked product that&rsquo;s <em>fixable</em> beats a product that never
    ships &mdash; is exactly what these demos address&hellip; Let&rsquo;s
    remember this is OUR product. Let&rsquo;s leave this class with no regrets
    and finish the strongest we can.&rdquo;
  </blockquote>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/cpHLzK5QXP4"
      title="First rough end-to-end demo for stakeholder feedback"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/model-use.png"
      alt="Terminal showing raw OTel spans (llm_call spans with models gemini-2.5-flash-lite and gemini-3-flash-preview and their token counts) next to Gemini CLI's model-usage summary table."
    />
    <figcaption>
      A quirk I found here: with model set to <em>Auto</em>, Gemini CLI resolves
      aliases internally, so its summary can name different models than the raw
      OTel spans. Selecting a model manually makes them line up &mdash; the raw
      telemetry is the ground truth.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Worth a footnote: this mismatch later <strong>resolved itself after a Gemini
    CLI update</strong> &mdash; the models now line up with the telemetry even on
    Auto &mdash; which suggests the CLI&rsquo;s end-of-session summary had simply
    been reporting resolved aliases that the OTel payload hadn&rsquo;t caught up
    to yet. Either way, keying off the raw telemetry was the right call.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/model-match.png"
      alt="IssueSight sidebar and terminal after a Gemini CLI update: the sidebar's model list (gemini-3.1-flash-lite, gemini-3-flash-preview) now matches Gemini CLI's own end-of-session model-usage summary exactly."
    />
    <figcaption>
      After a later Gemini CLI update, the detected models match the CLI&rsquo;s
      own summary even on Auto &mdash; the earlier discrepancy was on the
      telemetry side, not ours.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    This first cut linked issues to sessions by a manual &ldquo;claim,&rdquo; and
    intentionally skipped branch/commit-based issue detection &mdash; the goal was
    just to make the core idea (<em>a session works on an issue; AIT detects the
    model and tracks the tokens</em>) something a non-engineer could watch and
    immediately get. It embodied the agile principle I kept coming back to: a
    flawed-but-shippable release beats a perfect one that never ships.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the final presentation I took the demo further &mdash; replacing the
    manual claim with <strong>automatic branch detection</strong> and adding
    <strong> multi-session / multi-issue</strong> capability. A session reads the
    current git branch (named <code>issue-#</code>), parses the number, and links
    the matching issue:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/branch-detection.png"
      alt="Debug output showing the receiver capturing a session whose branch field reads 'active(feat/cohesive-logic-and-ui)', circled in red, alongside a live Gemini CLI session in that branch."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Automatic branch detection: the session picks up the active git branch and
      uses it to attribute work to the right issue &mdash; no manual claim needed.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/ait/token-track.png"
      alt="IssueSight sidebar UI: an issue claimed for the active agent session, showing model list, token usage (in/out/total), estimated cost, request time, and a live model-usage table matching the terminal output."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The final demo UI: branch/issue detection, live token tracking with cost,
      model detection, request duration, and commit tracking, all tied to the
      claimed issue.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/CGrZW1l389Y"
      title="Final IssueSight demo: branch detection, token/cost tracking, multi-session"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few things here are deliberate shortcuts for the presentation &mdash; branch
    linking uses a local <code>issue-#</code> naming convention rather than
    GitHub&rsquo;s native branch-issue links, and cost is hard-coded &mdash; but it
    showcases the full vision: branch/issue detection, token tracking with cost,
    model detection, request duration, commit tracking, multi-session workflows,
    and the UI. To hand this cleanly to a teammate to build on, I kept the demo
    logic isolated in its own branch and wrote up every file I touched and the
    functions (<code>t2getAllActiveSessions()</code>,
    <code>t2getActiveSession(sessionId)</code>) they&rsquo;d need to pull session
    data.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- SCOPE / HONEST MVP ---------------- */}
  <h2>An honest note on scope</h2>
  <p>
    Our original vision had more moving parts than we ultimately shipped &mdash;
    persisting sessions to a SQLite database being the clearest example. Over the
    ten weeks, with issues slipping and some communication friction across an
    eleven-person team, we naturally converged on what we could realistically
    deliver: a <strong>RAM-first MVP</strong> that tracked everything live from
    the in-memory session map, which was enough to demonstrate the core value and
    get real feedback. The idea of tracking live from RAM before writing to SQLite
    actually surfaced early &mdash; partly to avoid the performance cost of hitting
    the database on every LLM call &mdash; and it became the pragmatic path once we
    took an honest look at our timeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the MVP itself moved throughout the project. This is the point of building iteratively:
    scope should bend to reality, and a working slice you can actually show beats an ambitious
    plan that never ships. The database, native GitHub branch-issue linking, and precise cost
    modeling all remain clear post-MVP next steps rather than things I&rsquo;d claim
    we finished.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- REFLECTION ---------------- */}
  <h2>What I took away</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Design the contract before the code.</strong> Agreeing on the
      <code>OTelSpan</code>/<code>SessionContext</code> shapes and the
      <code>SessionManager</code> signatures up front let two people build two
      halves of a system in parallel without them drifting apart.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ground truth beats self-report.</strong> Reaching for OTel and hooks
      instead of asking the model to describe itself is what made the numbers
      trustworthy &mdash; and validating them against the CLI&rsquo;s own summary
      proved it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ship something watchable.</strong> A rough demo unblocked stakeholder
      feedback faster than a polished build would have &mdash; and repeatedly
      pulled the team&rsquo;s vision back into focus.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ProjectLayout>
